# Build output and caches
.next
node_modules
coverage
tsconfig.tsbuildinfo
.vercel
.repo-git
npm-debug.log*

# Secrets. The catch-all is what actually protects us -- the specific entries
# above it were the ones written before it existed. `.env.example` is the one
# file here that must stay tracked, so it is negated explicitly rather than
# relying on the fact that git keeps already-tracked files: without this line,
# removing and re-adding it would silently drop it from the repo.
.env*
!.env.example

# Test output. Playwright rewrites these on every run and the cards dealt are
# random, so the bytes differ each time -- they are regenerable output, not
# layout specs. Tracking them meant `git status` was never clean after a run.
test-results
playwright-report
artifacts/screenshots/

# Scratch output from the Playwright MCP server (console dumps, page snapshots).
.playwright-mcp/

# Bundled 3D-room artifact (scripts/build-game3d-artifact.mjs)
dist-artifact/

# Intermediate masks from scripts/build-avatar-sprites.sh
scripts/.avatar-masks/

# One-off diagnostic scripts (Firefox checks, screenshot shims)
scripts/tmp/

# Uncompressed 3D source assets (~50MB). scripts/compress-3d-assets.sh reads
# these and writes the ~3.8MB derivatives in public/models and public/props,
# which ARE tracked because they are what ships.
#
# Untracked deliberately, and it is a real trade: a fresh clone cannot re-run
# the pipeline without fetching the sources from somewhere. The alternative was
# putting 50MB of binaries in every clone of this repo forever, to support a
# script that is run when an artist delivers a new model and at no other time.
# The sources currently live on the local feature/3d-poker-simulation branch
# and in ../PokerGame-model-sources; neither is a durable home, and picking one
# (object storage, LFS, or simply pushing that branch) is still an open call.
assets-src/
.tmp*.mjs
.tmp3d/
scripts/__pycache__/
